instructions:
You are working in the JavaScript repository checked out at /luxon (the luxon date library, source in /luxon/src, tests in /luxon/test run with jest via `npx jest` or `npm test`).

GOAL: Fix a bug in /luxon/src/interval.js so that the behaviour named by the test `Interval.fromISO works with Settings.throwOnInvalid` passes. That test is NOT present in the repo; its name describes the expected behaviour: when `Settings.throwOnInvalid` is true, `Interval.fromISO(...)` should throw an `InvalidIntervalError` for unparsable input rather than letting an `InvalidDateTimeError` escape from the inner `DateTime.fromISO` calls.

CURRENT CODE (around line 134 of /luxon/src/interval.js):
```js
  static fromISO(text, opts) {
    const [s, e] = (text || "").split("/", 2);
    if (s && e) {
      const start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts),
        end = DateTime.fromISO(e, opts);

      if (start.isValid && end.isValid) {
        return Interval.fromDateTimes(start, end);
      }

      if (start.isValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(e, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.after(start, dur);
        }
      } else if (end.isValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(s, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.before(end, dur);
        }
      }
    }
    return Interval.invalid("unparsable", `the input "${text}" can't be parsed asISO 8601`);
  }
```
With `Settings.throwOnInvalid = true`, `DateTime.fromISO(s, opts)` throws `InvalidDateTimeError` as soon as one side is unparsable, so `Interval.fromISO` never reaches its own `Interval.invalid(...)` call and the wrong error type escapes. Also `Duration.fromISO(e, opts)` can throw the same way when the other side is a duration string.

WHAT TO DO:
1. Read /luxon/src/interval.js and /luxon/src/errors.js, /luxon/src/settings.js, /luxon/test/interval/*.js to understand the surrounding conventions.
2. Fix `Interval.fromISO` so that the DateTime/Duration parsing of each side is done defensively (e.g. wrap each `DateTime.fromISO(...)`/`Duration.fromISO(...)` in try/catch and treat a thrown error as "not valid"), so that the method always ends up calling `Interval.invalid("unparsable", ...)` when nothing can be parsed. Keep the successful paths (start/end, start/duration, duration/end) working exactly as before when `throwOnInvalid` is false. Do not change any other behaviour, do not add features, and do not touch any test file.
3. Note the message string currently contains a typo `asISO 8601`; the correct English is `as ISO 8601`. Check whether any pre-existing test asserts the exact string; if none does, fix the typo, otherwise leave it as-is. Report what you found.
4. Verify with commands (from /luxon): `npx jest test/interval` and the full suite `npx jest`. All pre-existing tests must still pass. Also write a quick throwaway check (e.g. a temp file outside the repo test dir or `node -e` with a small script, or a temporary jest test that you DELETE afterwards) proving that with `Settings.throwOnInvalid = true`, `Interval.fromISO("not a date/also not a date")` throws an error whose name is `InvalidIntervalError`, and that with `Settings.throwOnInvalid = false` it returns an invalid Interval with reason "unparsable".

CONSTRAINTS: Only edit /luxon/src/interval.js (plus, if strictly needed, nothing else). Never edit or delete files under /luxon/test. Do not run `npm install` or fetch anything from the network. Do not leave any temporary files behind.

REPORT BACK: the exact diff you applied, the commands you ran, the pass/fail counts of the full jest suite before and after, whether the typo was safe to fix, and any residual uncertainty.

