## Bug fixed: `DateTime#isInDST()` for 1974 USA (Jan 6 – Oct 27)

### Exact diff (`/luxon/src/datetime.js`, only change I made)
```diff
@@ -1166,7 +1166,8 @@ export default class DateTime {
       return false;
     } else {
       return (
-        this.offset > this.set({ month: 1 }).offset || this.offset > this.set({ month: 5 }).offset
+        this.offset > this.set({ month: 1, day: 1 }).offset ||
+        this.offset > this.set({ month: 5, day: 1 }).offset
       );
     }
   }
```

### Root cause
`isInDST` compared the current offset against the offset of the *same day-of-month* in January and May. In 1974 US DST ran Jan 6 02:00 → Oct 27 02:00, so for any date whose day-of-month is ≥ 6, `set({ month: 1 })` lands on a date that is *itself* DST (offset −240), so `this.offset > that` is false; the May reference is always DST too. Only days 1–5 accidentally worked. Anchoring the references to `day: 1` puts the January reference on Jan 1 (standard time, offset −300) which is a valid standard-time baseline for 1974, while May 1 remains the standard-time baseline for the southern hemisphere. No special-casing of 1974.

### Reproduction (before fix, `America/New_York`)
```
1974-01-05T12:00 offset=-300 isInDST=false  (correct)
1974-01-06T12:00 offset=-240 isInDST=false  (EXPECTED true)  <-- bug
1974-06-15T12:00 offset=-240 isInDST=false  (EXPECTED true)  <-- bug
1974-10-26T12:00 offset=-240 isInDST=false  (EXPECTED true)  <-- bug
1974-10-27T12:00 offset=-300 isInDST=false  (correct)
```
